989775658, "top": [["4", -0.0007209395989775658], ["2", -9.934444427490234]]}, {"t": "\"", "lp": -0.00023671805683989078, "top": [["\"", -0.00023671805683989078], [" \"", -11.057286262512207]]}, {"t": " |", "lp": -0.3687317967414856, "top": [[" |", -0.3687317967414856], [" >", -1.1784766912460327]]}, {"t": " sudo", "lp": -0.04492513835430145, "top": [[" sudo", -0.04492513835430145], [" tee", -3.1360220909118652]]}, {"t": " tee", "lp": -0.00014604204625356942, "top": [[" tee", -0.00014604204625356942], [" te", -9.075008392333984]]}, {"t": " /", "lp": -0.000293476739898324, "top": [[" /", -0.000293476739898324], [" -", -8.35708999633789]]}, {"t": "etc", "lp": -3.111410842393525e-05, "top": [["etc", -3.111410842393525e-05], ["tmp", -11.35922908782959]]}, {"t": "/mod", "lp": -0.0918167233467102, "top": [["/mod", -0.0918167233467102], ["/modules", -2.615105628967285]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are distributed across the GPU's compute engines. This is set via kernel module parameters for the `xe` driver.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 1** \u2014 *Balanced/Default*\n- Distributes CCS contexts across compute engines in a way that balances load\n- Generally the recommended default for most workloads\n- Good general-purpose behavior\n\n**ccs_mode 2** \u2014 *Alternative distribution*\n- Uses a different mapping strategy for CCS contexts\n- May perform better in specific scenarios depending on your workload\n\n**ccs_mode 4** \u2014 *Single CCS / dedicated*\n- Pins contexts to specific compute slices\n- Useful when you want **deterministic** or **isolated** compute behavior\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Use Case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Default; general workloads, best all-around choice |\n| **2** | Experiment when mode 1 shows issues; some specific kernels |\n| **4** | When you need deterministic CCS assignment, isolation, or are debugging compute scheduling issues |\n\n## Practical Notes\n\n- **Most users should stick with mode 1** (or leave it unset, which typically defaults to a sensible value)\n- These modes matter most for **compute-heavy workloads** (ML training/inference, HPC), not general desktop use\n- Differences are often subtle and workload-dependent\n- You can check your current mode with:\n  ```bash\n  cat /sys/module/xe/parameters/ccs_mode\n  ```\n\n## Setting It\n\nTo set at boot (e.g., mode 4):\n```bash\necho \"options xe ccs_mode=4\" | sudo tee /etc/mod"}